// Practice quiz: Flexbox
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/html-and-css/flexbox

::o1 direction without flex::A ul has flex-direction row in its CSS but no display rule. Its li items stack top to bottom. Why? {
=flex-direction only works on a flex container, so the ul needs display flex #Correct. Without display flex, the list is a normal block and flex-direction has no effect.
~li elements can never sit side by side #Not quite. Any elements can be laid out in a row by a flex container.
~flex-direction row stacks items vertically #Not quite. row is horizontal; column stacks.
~The items need flex-direction too #Not quite. flex-direction belongs on the container, not the items.
}

::o1 which elements move::A div has display flex. Which elements become flex items? {
=Only its direct children #Correct. Flex layout applies to the container's direct children; grandchildren keep their normal layout.
~Every element inside it, at any depth #Not quite. Only direct children are flex items.
~The div itself #Not quite. The div becomes the flex container, not an item.
~Only children that also have display flex #Not quite. Direct children become flex items whatever their own display is.
}

::o2 vertical centring in a row::In a flex row, which property centres the items vertically? {
=align-items center #Correct. In a row the cross axis is vertical, and align-items works on the cross axis.
~justify-content center #Not quite. In a row, justify-content works on the horizontal main axis.
~gap center #Not quite. gap only sets space between items and takes a length.
~flex-direction column #Not quite. That changes the direction instead of centring.
}

::o2 gap at the edges::A flex row has gap 20px and three items. Where does the 20px appear? {
=Only between neighbouring items, twice in total #Correct. gap adds space between items, never before the first or after the last.
~Between items and at both outer edges #Not quite. gap never adds space at the container's edges; that is padding.
~Only after the last item #Not quite. gap sits between each pair of neighbours.
~Around every item, like a margin #Not quite. gap is between items only.
}

::o3 sharing leftover space::A 500px flex container holds two items with flex-basis 100px. Item A has flex-grow 3 and item B has flex-grow 1. How wide is A? {
=325px #Correct. Leftover is 500 - 200 \= 300px; A gets three quarters, 225px, plus its 100px basis.
~375px #Not quite. That gives A all of three quarters of the whole container instead of the leftover plus its basis.
~300px #Not quite. That is the leftover, not A's share of it.
~250px #Not quite. That splits the space equally, ignoring the 3 to 1 grow ratio.
}
